0.0\" cy=\"395\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#b86a0a\"/><text x=\"183\" y=\"403\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#b86a0a\">Half step: C to C\u266f</text><path d=\"M90.0 450 H230.0\" stroke=\"#2e6475\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/><circle cx=\"90.0\" cy=\"450\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#2e6475\"/><text x=\"258.0\" y=\"458\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#2e6475\">Whole step: C to D (two half steps)</text><path d=\"M390.0 505 H530.0\" stroke=\"#2e6475\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/><circle cx=\"390.0\" cy=\"505\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#2e6475\"/><text x=\"558.0\" y=\"513\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#2e6475\">Half step: E to F (no black key between)</text></svg></div>\n<div class=\"card\" style=\"margin-top:20px;padding:20px 24px;font-size:22px;line-height:1.4\">A <b>half step</b> is the very next key, black or white. A <b>sharp \u266f</b> raises a note by a half step, and a <b>flat \u266d</b> lowers it by one. E\u2013F and B\u2013C are half steps with no black key between them.</div><div class=\"foot\"><span>Pianoers.com piano basics</span><b>PIANOERS.COM</b></div></body></html>"]}